WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.223
The payments company keeps its payments on ten machines.

2
00:00:06.223 --> 00:00:09.305
Each payment lives on machine number hash mod 10.

3
00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:16.485
Turn the payment's id into a big random-looking number, its hash, divide by 10, and keep the remainder.

4
00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:22.351
The busiest shopping day of the year is a week away, and an eleventh machine has just arrived.

5
00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:29.209
The plan fits on one line: switch to hash mod 11, and copy every payment whose answer changed.

6
00:00:29.209 --> 00:00:31.027
So how many payments move?

7
00:00:31.027 --> 00:00:32.147
Hold your guess.

8
00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:37.534
The new machine needs only its fair share, one payment in eleven.

9
00:00:37.534 --> 00:00:40.829
Out of 100,000 payments, that's about 9,100.

10
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:44.276
Now count what hash mod 11 actually moves.

11
00:00:44.276 --> 00:00:44.811
91,032.

12
00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:52.223
Nine payments in ten pack up and leave, and most of them just go from one old machine to another.

13
00:00:52.467 --> 00:00:57.508
It's like hiring one new cashier and making every cashier in the shop swap tills.

14
00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:04.184
On three terabytes of payments, that's 2.7 terabytes of copying, when 270 gigabytes would do.

15
00:01:04.184 --> 00:01:09.455
Wouldn't it be nice if the new machine took its share, and nothing else moved?

16
00:01:09.933 --> 00:01:12.977
Picture the left-luggage office at a big station.

17
00:01:12.977 --> 00:01:17.200
A thousand numbered lockers, ten attendants, a hundred lockers each.

18
00:01:17.467 --> 00:01:21.868
A traveller's locker number is stamped on their key, and it never changes.

19
00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:29.390
When an eleventh attendant starts, the manager hands her the keys to 90 lockers, about nine from each of the others.

20
00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:32.643
No locker moves, and no traveller gets a new number.

21
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:36.661
Let's shrink it to twelve lockers and three attendants, and map it.

22
00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:40.524
Each locker is a partition: a fixed bucket of payments.

23
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.116
Each attendant is a machine, and from here on we'll call a machine a node.

24
00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:49.917
The number on the key is hash mod 12, worked out once and never changed.

25
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.793
And the manager's list is a table: which node looks after which partition.

26
00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:59.639
The payment pay-7 hashes to partition 9, which lives on node A.

27
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:05.048
And pay-81701 is in partition 2, on node C.

28
00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:06.460
Now node D joins.

29
00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.712
How many partitions should it get?

30
00:02:08.712 --> 00:02:11.959
And how many payments get a new partition number?

31
00:02:11.959 --> 00:02:13.019
Pause and guess.

32
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.869
Three partitions: twelve, split four ways.

33
00:02:18.869 --> 00:02:23.190
And not one payment gets a new number, because twelve didn't change.

34
00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:25.351
Only the table is about to change.

35
00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:31.047
They're all tied at four, so A goes first, and hands over its highest partition, p9.

36
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:35.808
A copies p9 to D, and keeps serving payments from it while it copies.

37
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:39.733
When D has caught up, the table says p9 lives on D.

38
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.858
Then B gives p10, and C gives p11.

39
00:02:42.858 --> 00:02:44.792
Every node holds three.

40
00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:47.333
Check both payments.

41
00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:52.547
pay-81701 is still in p2, and p2 never left C.

42
00:02:52.833 --> 00:02:55.955
pay-7 is still in p9, which now lives on D.

43
00:02:55.955 --> 00:02:59.439
It moved, but only as part of a whole partition.

44
00:02:59.733 --> 00:03:05.589
Three partitions of twelve moved: a quarter, D's fair share, and three nodes shared the sending.

45
00:03:05.589 --> 00:03:10.712
Hash mod N, going from three nodes to four, would have moved three payments in four.

46
00:03:11.167 --> 00:03:13.196
Here the lockers picture breaks.

47
00:03:13.196 --> 00:03:15.351
Handing over a key takes a second.

48
00:03:15.351 --> 00:03:24.925
Moving a partition means copying gigabytes between machines that are busy serving payments, and the copying loads the very nodes it's meant to relieve.

49
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.352
The new attendant isn't handed keys.

50
00:03:27.352 --> 00:03:31.656
She's handed the luggage, by colleagues who are still serving the queue.

51
00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:42.389
At full size, a thousand partitions on ten nodes, the eleventh node takes 90, and only 8,955 of the 100,000 payments move.

52
00:03:42.389 --> 00:03:47.196
All of them go to the new node, sent by all ten old ones.

53
00:03:47.467 --> 00:03:52.188
Moving data so every node holds a fair share is called rebalancing.

54
00:03:52.467 --> 00:03:59.215
And this design is called fixed partitions: far more partitions than nodes, chosen once, and moved whole.

55
00:03:59.533 --> 00:04:05.876
Kafka, a program that keeps logs of messages, splits each log into a fixed number of partitions.

56
00:04:06.167 --> 00:04:09.582
And Notion, the notes app, runs 480 shards.

57
00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:16.015
Going from 32 machines to 96, whole shards moved, and no workspace changed shard.

58
00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:21.091
The catch is that the number of partitions, call it P, is chosen once.

59
00:04:21.091 --> 00:04:23.255
Pick too few, and you hit a wall.

60
00:04:23.533 --> 00:04:27.581
With twelve partitions, a thirteenth node has nothing to hold.

61
00:04:27.581 --> 00:04:29.605
It gets a chair and no lockers.

62
00:04:29.867 --> 00:04:36.295
Pick too many, and every partition's fixed cost adds up: its own files, its own line in the table.

63
00:04:36.567 --> 00:04:42.696
And changing P later means rehashing the keys, which is the hash mod N mess all over again.

64
00:04:43.567 --> 00:04:46.388
So pick a P with plenty of divisors.

65
00:04:46.388 --> 00:04:51.089
Notion chose 480 for the same reason an hour has 60 minutes.

66
00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:56.120
480 spreads evenly over 32, 40, 48 or 96 machines.

67
00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:57.261
512 doesn't.

68
00:04:57.261 --> 00:05:06.768
On 96 machines, some hold six and some hold five, and the busiest carries 13% more than the average.

69
00:05:07.233 --> 00:05:10.266
So, how much should one new machine disturb?

70
00:05:10.266 --> 00:05:11.300
Only its share.

71
00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:16.071
Number the lockers once, hand them over whole, and change only the list.

72
00:05:16.071 --> 00:05:19.324
Pick P once, for the size you expect years from now.

73
00:05:19.833 --> 00:05:21.281
One quick check.

74
00:05:21.281 --> 00:05:24.447
A fifth node, E, joins our cluster.

75
00:05:24.447 --> 00:05:28.247
Does pay-81701 get a new partition number?

76
00:05:28.247 --> 00:05:29.695
Pause and think.

77
00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:33.157
No.

78
00:05:33.157 --> 00:05:35.217
It stays in p2, forever.

79
00:05:35.217 --> 00:05:40.024
At most, p2 gets a new node, and only the table changes.
